        <div id="third-party" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Sprouts Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No third-party testing is published for Sprouts protein powder. Neither the Sprouts Whey Protein nor the Sprouts Vegan Protein label or online listing mentions NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport, Informed Choice or a Clean Label Project result, and Sprouts does not publish lot test results for its house supplements. The vegan line's non-GMO and gluten-free claims are about ingredients, not about contaminants or label accuracy.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not mean the powders are unsafe. It means you are relying on the contract manufacturer's quality control and on FDA rules for dietary supplements, which do not require pre-market testing. Store brands are almost always made by outside manufacturers, and the label only says the powder is made exclusively for Sprouts Farmers Market, without naming the maker.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant powders draw extra attention for heavy metals, because pea and rice take up lead and cadmium from soil. Independent groups have found measurable levels in many plant protein powders, including big brands, usually below legal limits but higher than typical whey. Sprouts publishes no heavy-metal results for its vegan powder. If that worries you, our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> lists brands that publish results.
          </p>

          <p>
            Who needs a certified powder? Competitive athletes who are drug-tested should only buy products with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, and nothing in the Sprouts house range qualifies. The good news is that Sprouts sells one that does: Garden of Life Sport Organic Plant-Based Protein, which Garden of Life lists as NSF Certified for Sport. Our <a href="/garden-of-life-protein-powder-review/">Garden of Life protein review</a> explains which of that brand's NSF badges actually means sport testing. For everyone else, our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> covers what to look for.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Non-GMO and clean-label are not the same as tested.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A non-GMO or gluten-free claim describes the ingredients. NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport means the finished powder was checked for banned substances. If you are drug-tested, only the second counts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="name-brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Should You Buy Garden of Life, Orgain or Vega at Sprouts Instead?
